Disabled Logging Misconfiguration Scenario — Firewall Counter + Log-Trail Absence Check
Method: iptables -L INPUT -n -v counters sampled before/after traffic generation, plus dmesg/journalctl checked directly for any netfilter LOG entries matching this flow. There is no LOG rule in this scenario by design -- that absence is the misconfiguration itself.

--- Checkpoint 1: Before any traffic ---
[2026-09-13 18:35:05]
DROP rule: pkts=0   bytes=0

--- Traffic generated: 10 curl attempts from h1 to h2:8000 (2026-09-13 18:35:07 to 2026-09-13 18:35:37) ---

--- Checkpoint 2: After traffic ---
[2026-09-13 18:35:39]
DROP rule: pkts=20   bytes=1200
Delta: +20 packets (traffic WAS blocked)

--- Log trail check (confirming NO audit trail exists for this blocked traffic) ---
dmesg | grep -iE 'SRC=10.0.0.1|DPT=8000|ACL_BLOCK':
    (no matching lines -- confirms no netfilter LOG entries for this flow)
journalctl -k --no-pager -n 200 | grep -iE 'SRC=10.0.0.1|DPT=8000|ACL_BLOCK':
    (no matching lines)
journalctl -k raw sample (context on journal availability in this environment):
    [0;1;39mNo journal files were found.[0m
-- No entries --

Conclusion: 20 packets were confirmed dropped above, yet zero matching log entries exist in dmesg or the kernel journal -- this is the disabled-logging misconfiguration.
